<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Ravensburg</h1><p class="st-meta">1276 CE – 1802 CE (approx.) · capital Ravensburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Upper Swabian imperial city that was home to the Great Ravensburg Trading Company, one of the leading merchant houses of late medieval Europe.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Ravensburg grew below the Veitsburg, ancestral castle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">Guelph</a> dukes, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-the-lion">Henry the Lion</a> may have been born. The town passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> with the Guelph inheritance. After their line ended it became an imperial city, recognised by Rudolf I around 1276.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A council of patricians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> governed. From about 1380 to 1530 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-ravensburg-trading-company">Great Ravensburg Trading Company</a>, owned by local families, traded linen, fustian and saffron across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/county-of-flanders">Flanders</a>. Paper mills made the town an early centre of German papermaking. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> it became one of the few bi-confessional imperial cities, with Catholics and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutherans</a> sharing offices under the parity of 1648.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Ravensburg lost its freedom in 1802 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and passed to Württemberg in 1810. It is now a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, known for its towers and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravensburger">Ravensburger</a> games company.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="imperial city, 1276 CE – 1802 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">imperial city</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Germany and Central Europe</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:85.09%;width:10.47%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1276 CE – 1802 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>imperial city</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Ravensburg</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism, Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swabian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ravensburg</dd></dl></aside></div>
